,{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-01bb414fc741582ab547f3300c36b8c1.mjs","model":{"question":"For a dataset, $r = 0.05$. Can we conclude there is no relationship at all between the two variables?","choices":[{"correct":false,"text":"Yes—r near 0 means the variables are unrelated","feedback":"r near 0 rules out only a linear relationship—not any relationship."},{"correct":true,"text":"No—r only measures linear relationships; a strong curved relationship could still exist","feedback":"Correct! r measures only linear relationships. The variables could still have a strong curvilinear relationship, which is why we must also look at the scatterplot."},{"correct":false,"text":"Yes, as long as r is positive","feedback":"A small r does not need to be negative to indicate weak linear association."}],"multi":false,"hint":"What kind of relationship does r measure?","explanation":""},"key":"Txss7V8spY"}],"key":"UsFZPXX7gQ"}],"key":"K0ufYHkVsC"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"The Correlation Coefficient r","url":"/pages/p053-m05-linear-relationships-2-of-8","group":"Section 4: Examining Relationships"},"next":{"title":"Cautions About Correlation: Linearity, Units, and Outliers","url":"/pages/p055-m05-linear-relationships-4-of-8","group":"Section 4: Examining Relationships"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3000"}